# The FH Language

FH is a small, dynamically-typed scripting language designed to be embedded
inside native applications (particularly game engines). It compiles source
to bytecode and runs on a register-based virtual machine with an automatic
mark-and-sweep garbage collector. The whole implementation is C99, ~10k
lines, with no external runtime dependencies.

This document has two parts:

1. **Language basics** — syntax, types, control flow, functions.
2. **Standard library reference** — every built-in function, grouped by
   category, with parameters, return values and short examples.

For build instructions and the embedding API, see [`README.md`](../README.md)
and [`src/fh.h`](../src/fh.h).

---

## 1. Language basics

### 1.1 Comments

```
# this is a single-line comment, runs to the end of the line

#-
  this is a block comment.
  it can span multiple lines.
-#
```

### 1.2 Values and types

FH is dynamically typed: variables don't carry a type, only values do.
There are eight kinds of value:

- `null` — the absence of a value.
- `boolean` — `true` or `false`. Only `null` and `false` are falsy; every
  other value (including `0` and `""`) is truthy.
- `number` — FH has two numeric subtypes, **integer** and **float**. An
  integer literal (`10`) produces an integer value; a literal with a
  decimal point or exponent (`10.0`, `1e3`) produces a float. Most
  arithmetic promotes to float when either operand is a float.
- `string` — immutable byte sequences, written with single or double
  quotes: `"hello"`, `'hello'`.
- `array` — an ordered, growable list of values of any type:
  `[1, "two", [3]]`.
- `map` — a hash map keyed by any non-null value:
  `{"a": 1, true: "yes"}`.
- `closure` (function) — first-class; functions defined with `fn` are
  values and can be stored, passed and returned like any other value.
- `c_obj` — an opaque handle around host (C) data, used by bindings such
  as the file/tar I/O functions below. Scripts can't construct one
  directly; the standard library hands them out (e.g. `io_open`).

Use `type(x)` to get a value's type name as a string, and `has()` (below) to
locate a value inside an array or map.

### 1.3 Variables

Declare a variable with `let`:

```
let x = 10;
let name = "FH";
let nothing;        # implicitly null
```

`let` inside a function creates a local, block-scoped variable. `let` at
the top level of a script creates a global that every function in the
script can read (see `tests/test_globals.fh`).

By convention (not enforced by the language) an `ALL_CAPS` name signals
"treat this as a constant" — FH has no dedicated `const` keyword.

### 1.4 Operators

```
Arithmetic:   +  -  *  /  %
Comparison:   ==  !=  <  >  <=  >=
Logical:      &&  ||  !
Bitwise:      &  |  ^  ~  <<  >>
Increment:    ++x  x++  --x  x--   (prefix and postfix)
Assignment:   =
String concat: +          (e.g. "a" + "b" == "ab")
Optional index: ?.[...]   (see 1.9)
```

There is no ternary `?:` operator.

When one side of `+` is a string, the other is rendered into it: a number,
a boolean (`"true"` / `"false"`) or `null` (`"null"`, the same word `print`
and `json_stringify` use). Anything else — an array, a map, a closure — is
an error, because there is no sensible text for it:

```
"n = " + 3          # "n = 3"
"ok? " + false      # "ok? false"
"err: " + null      # "err: null"     <- what a missing field reads as
"x" + [1, 2]        # error: string addition with unsupported type array
```

### 1.5 Control flow

```
if (x > 0) {
    ...
} elif (x == 0) {
    ...
} else {
    ...
}

while (x < 10) {
    x = x + 1;
}

# repeat/until executes the block at least once, then keeps looping
# for as long as the condition stays TRUE (it stops the moment the
# condition becomes false) -- this is the opposite convention from
# Lua's "repeat ... until", so don't assume the two behave the same.
let a = 0;
repeat {
    a = a + 1;
} until (a < 5);   # loops while a < 5, so this ends with a == 5

for (let i = 0; i < 10; i = i + 1) {
    if (i == 5) { continue; }
    if (i == 9) { break; }
}
```

Any of the three `for` clauses may be omitted (`for (; i < 10; i = i + 1)`).

### 1.6 Functions and closures

```
fn add(a, b) {
    return a + b;
}

# default parameter values
fn greet(name, greeting = "Hello") {
    printf("%s, %s!\n", greeting, name);
}
greet("Alice");        # "Hello, Alice!"
greet("Bob", "Hi");     # "Hi, Bob!"

# functions are values, and closures capture their enclosing scope
fn make_counter(start) {
    return {
        "next": fn() { start = start + 1; },
        "read": fn() { return start; },
    };
}
let c = make_counter(0);
c.next();
c.next();
print(c.read());   # 2
```

A function definition can carry a docstring, retrievable with
`docstring(fn)`:

```
fn area(w, h) : "computes the area of a rectangle" {
    return w * h;
}
docstring(area);   # "computes the area of a rectangle"
```

### 1.7 Arrays and maps

```
let a = [1, "two", [3, 4]];
a[0];                 # 1
append(a, "five");    # appends in place, also returns the array
len(a);                # 4
delete(a, 0);          # removes index 0, returns the removed value

let m = {"name": "FH", "version": 1};
m.name;                 # "FH"        (dot access, when the key is an identifier)
m["version"];           # 1           (bracket access, works for any key type)
m.new_key = true;       # adding a new key
contains_key(m, "name"); # true
delete(m, "name");       # removes the key, returns the removed value
```

`len()` and `append()` are compiler intrinsics (compiled to dedicated
bytecode, not ordinary function calls) and work on arrays, maps and
strings (`len` only) — see section 2 for the rest of the array/map helpers
(`has`, `reset`, `next_key`, `reserve`, ...).

### 1.8 String interpolation

`${expr}` inside a string literal is expanded at compile time into string
concatenation:

```
let name = "Alice";
let age = 30;
print("Hello, ${name}! You are ${age} years old.");
# same as: "Hello, " + name + "! You are " + age + " years old."
```

### 1.9 Optional chaining

`?.[key]` performs the same lookup as `[key]`, but short-circuits to
`null` (instead of erroring) when the value being indexed is `null`:

```
let user = {"profile": {"city": "NYC"}};
user?.["profile"]?.["city"];     # "NYC"
let missing = null;
missing?.["profile"];             # null, no error
```

### 1.10 Errors

`error("message")` aborts the running script with an error (see it used
throughout the standard library — most built-ins fail this way instead of
crashing). `assert(cond)` / `assert(cond, "message")` raises an error if
`cond` is falsy. FH programs get a full stack trace on error:

```
ERROR: file.fh:5:14: error: division by zero

Traceback (most recent call last):
  File "file.fh", line 33, in main
  File "file.fh", line 20, in process_data
  File "file.fh", line 5, in divide
```

`error(x)` takes any value, not only a string: `error(404)` and
`error({"code": 404})` render the value into the message.

**Catching one — `pcall(f [, args...])`.** FH has no `try`/`catch`. What it
has is a protected call: `pcall` runs `f` and comes back either way, so a
failure costs you that one call instead of the program.

```
let r = pcall(load_level, "level3.json");
if (r.ok) {
    start(r.value);
} else {
    println("could not load the level: " + r.error);
    start(default_level());
}
```

It always returns a map, and `ok` says which shape it is:

| Key | On success | On failure |
|---|---|---|
| `ok` | `true` | `false` |
| `value` | what `f` returned | `null` |
| `error` | `null` | the message, as a string |
| `file`, `line`, `col` | absent | where it was raised |
| `traceback` | absent | the same stack trace the interpreter would have printed |

Notes:

- `f` may be a named function, an anonymous one, or a **C function** the host
  registered — a binding that fails is caught exactly like a script error,
  which is the case that matters when FH is embedded in an engine.
- To protect an *expression* rather than a call, wrap it:
  `pcall(fn() { return risky() + 1; })`.
- `pcall` nests. An inner `pcall` that catches something does not stop an
  outer one from catching what happens next.
- Errors in `pcall`'s own arguments — `pcall(42)` — are ordinary errors, and
  are themselves catchable by an outer `pcall`.
- There is no `finally`. Anything that has to be undone has to be undone by
  the caller, after looking at `ok`.

### 1.11 Multi-file scripts

```
include "helpers.fh";
```

`include` pulls another `.fh` file's top-level declarations into the
current file at compile time; see `src/input.c` for the include and
`.fhpack` (packaged/tar'd script bundle) machinery.

`include` is textual: the file's tokens are spliced in where the directive
appears, and every declaration lands in the same global namespace. There is
no `import` expression — `let m = import "mod.fh";` is not FH. What takes
its place is the object pattern in the next section: `include` the file,
then call the factory it declares.

### 1.12 Objects and modules

FH has no classes, but it has closures that capture their enclosing scope,
and maps that can hold functions. Those two together give you objects:
a function that builds a map of functions and returns it is a constructor,
and the locals it closes over are that object's private state.

```
fn Stack() {
    let items = [];            # private: never put on the map
    let count = 0;

    let stack = {};

    stack.push = fn(v) {
        items[count] = v;
        count = count + 1;
    };

    stack.pop = fn() {
        if (count == 0) { return null; }
        count = count - 1;
        return items[count];
    };

    stack.size = fn() { return count; };

    return stack;
}

let a = Stack();
let b = Stack();
a.push(1);
a.push(2);
b.push(9);
print(a.pop());    # 2
print(a.size());   # 1
print(b.size());   # 1  - the two do not share anything
```

Three things fall out of this, and they are what make it worth using
rather than a bag of global functions:

- **Privacy is real.** `items` and `count` are locals. Nothing outside
  `Stack()` can name them, so the only way to touch the contents is
  through the functions that were put on the map.
- **Instances are independent.** Every call to the constructor makes a
  fresh set of locals, so two objects never tread on each other.
- **Methods are bound.** A method does not need to be handed the object
  it belongs to; it already closed over everything it needs. That is why
  `a.pop()` works and no `self` argument appears anywhere.

Closures capture the scope, not a snapshot, so a method may also refer to
a sibling method, or to the map itself, even if the assignment happens
later in the constructor — by the time the method runs, they are all
there:

```
fn Greeter(name) {
    let g = {};

    let punctuation = fn() {
        if (g.excited) { return "!"; }
        return ".";
    };

    g.excited = false;
    g.hello = fn() { return "Hello, " + name + punctuation(); };

    return g;
}

let g = Greeter("world");
print(g.hello());     # Hello, world.
g.excited = true;
print(g.hello());     # Hello, world!
```

#### One object per file

For anything longer than a page, give each concern its own file, with one
constructor named after it, and let the caller assemble them. The file
declares exactly one thing; everything else in it stays local.

```
# counter.fh
fn Counter(start) {
    let n = start;
    let c = {};
    c.next = fn() { n = n + 1; return n; };
    c.read = fn() { return n; };
    return c;
}
```

```
# report.fh
fn Report(app) {
    let report = {};

    # Private helper: not on the map, so not part of the interface.
    let line = fn(label, value) { return label + ": " + value + "\n"; };

    report.render = fn() {
        return line("hits", app.hits.read())
             + line("misses", app.misses.read());
    };

    return report;
}
```

```
# main.fh
include "counter.fh"
include "report.fh"

fn main() {
    let app = {};
    app.hits = Counter(0);
    app.misses = Counter(0);
    app.report = Report(app);      # captures `app`, so it sees later changes

    app.hits.next();
    app.hits.next();
    app.misses.next();

    print(app.report.render());
}
```

Passing the shared state into the constructor once — `Report(app)` — is
what keeps it out of every call afterwards. The constructor captures it,
and every method reads it live, so a field written after construction is
still visible to methods built before it.

A few conventions that keep this readable:

- Name the constructor after the file: `render.fh` declares `Render`,
  `physics.fh` declares `Physics`. `include` puts them all in one global
  namespace, so a name shared by two files is a silent collision.
- Put on the map only what callers should reach. Everything else is a
  local, and stays unreachable by construction.
- Build the objects in one place, in dependency order, and hang them off a
  single state map. Constructors that take that map see each other through
  it without needing to be given one another directly.
- `include` each file exactly once, at the top of the entry file. Because
  the splice is textual, including a file twice declares its functions
  twice.

