## 2. Standard library reference

Everything below is a global function, callable directly from any script
— there are no namespaces/modules, just naming conventions (`string_*`,
`math_*`, `io_*`, `os_*`, `json_*`). Parameter types are as accepted by
the C implementation in `src/c_funcs.c`; passing the wrong type raises a
script error rather than crashing (per `CLAUDE.md`'s binding-safety rules
for anyone extending this file).

### 2.1 Language intrinsics (not in the `fh_std_c_funcs` table)

These two are special-cased by the compiler (`src/compiler.c`) into
dedicated bytecode instructions, rather than being ordinary C functions —
that also means they can't be reassigned or passed around like other
built-ins.

| Function | Description |
|---|---|
| `len(x)` | Returns the length of an array, map or string as an integer. Errors if `x` isn't one of those. |
| `append(arr, val)` | Appends `val` to the end of array `arr` in place, and returns `arr`. Errors if `arr` isn't an array. |

```
let a = [1, 2];
append(a, 3);      # a is now [1, 2, 3]
len(a);              # 3
len("hello");        # 5
```

### 2.2 Core / misc

| Function | Description |
|---|---|
| `type(x)` | Returns the type of `x` as a string (`"null"`, `"boolean"`, `"integer"`, `"float"`, `"string"`, `"array"`, `"map"`, `"closure"`, `"c_func"`, `"c_obj"`, ...). |
| `tostring(x)` | Converts a number (int or float) to its string representation. |
| `tonumber(s)` | Parses string `s` as a float; returns `null` if it isn't a valid number. |
| `tointeger(x)` | Converts a number to an integer (truncating a float). |
| `has(container, value)` | Searches an array or map for `value` (O(n)). Returns `[found_value, index]` if found, `false` otherwise. |
| `contains_key(map, key)` | Returns `true`/`false` for whether `key` exists in `map`. |
| `next_key(map, key)` | Returns the map key that follows `key` in iteration order; pass `null` to get the first key. Used to iterate a map without `for-in`. |
| `delete(container, key_or_index)` | Removes an entry from an array (by index) or map (by key) and returns the removed value. |
| `reset(container)` | Empties an array or map in place (keeps the underlying allocation, avoiding a realloc on reuse). |
| `reserve(container, capacity)` | Pre-allocates capacity in an array or map and returns it. |
| `docstring(fn)` | Returns the docstring of a closure (`fn f() : "doc" {}`), or `""` if it has none. Only works on closures. |
| `getversion()` | Returns the FH version string (e.g. `"1.0.rc6"`). |
| `error(x)` | Aborts the script with `x` as the message. A string is used as-is; any other value is rendered into one. |
| `pcall(f[, args...])` | Calls `f` with `args`, catching an error instead of aborting. Returns a map: `{ok: true, value: ...}` or `{ok: false, error: "...", file, line, col, traceback}`. See 1.10. |
| `assert(cond[, msg])` | Raises an error if `cond` is falsy; `msg` is included in the error if given. |
| `print(...)` | Prints any number of values with no separators or trailing newline. |
| `println(...)` | Like `print`, but appends the caller's source location and a newline. |
| `printf(fmt, ...)` | C-style formatted print to stdout. Specifiers: `%d`/`%u`/`%x` (integer), `%f`/`%g` (float), `%s` (any value), `%%` (literal `%`). Unlike `string_format`, `%d`/`%u`/`%x` require an integer argument and `%f`/`%g` require a float — passing the wrong numeric subtype is a script error. |
| `eval(code, fn_name)` | Compiles `code` (a string of FH source) in a fresh, sandboxed sub-program and calls its function `fn_name` with no arguments, returning its result. No values are passed from the calling program into the sandboxed code. |

```
let m = {"a": 1, "b": 2};
let k = next_key(m, null);
while (k != null) {
    printf("%s -> ", k);
    k = next_key(m, k);
}
```

### 2.3 String functions (`string_*`)

| Function | Description |
|---|---|
| `string_slice(s, start[, end])` | Cuts `s` around `start` (and optionally `end`). With 2 args returns `[s[0:start], s[start:]]`; with 3 args returns `[s[0:start], s[start:end], s[end:]]`. |
| `string_split(s, delimiters)` | Splits `s` on any of the characters in `delimiters` (like C's `strtok`), returns an array of strings. Max 1024 pieces. |
| `string_upper(s)` / `string_lower(s)` | Returns an upper/lower-cased copy of `s`. |
| `string_find(s, needle)` | Returns the byte index of the first occurrence of `needle` in `s`, or `-1` if not found. |
| `string_match(s, pattern)` | Matches `s` against a regex `pattern` (see `src/regex/re.c`); returns the match index, or `null` if no match. |
| `string_reverse(s)` | Returns `s` reversed. |
| `string_substr(s, start, len)` | Returns the `len`-byte substring of `s` starting at byte `start`. |
| `string_char(x)` | A digit ⇄ character helper, **not general character indexing**: if `x` is a string, returns the integer value of its first character treated as an ASCII digit (`ord(x[0]) - ord('0')`); if `x` is an integer `0-9`, returns the 1-character string for that digit. |
| `string_trim(s)` | Returns `s` with leading/trailing whitespace removed. |
| `string_format(fmt, ...)` | Like `printf` but returns a string instead of printing. Specifiers: `%d`/`%u`/`%x` (integer), `%f`/`%g` (float), `%s`/`%c` (string), `%%`. |
| `string_join(sep, s1, s2, ...)` | Joins two or more strings with separator `sep` between them. |

```
string_split("a,b,c", ",");            # ["a", "b", "c"]
string_format("%s is %d", "x", 5);      # "x is 5"
string_slice("hello world", 5);          # ["hello", " world"]
```

### 2.4 Math functions (`math_*`)

Trigonometric/exponential functions take and return floats.

| Function | Description |
|---|---|
| `math_abs(x)` | Absolute value; preserves int vs float. |
| `math_ceil(x)` / `math_floor(x)` | Rounds up/down; integers pass through unchanged. |
| `math_clamp(x, min, max)` | Clamps `x` to `[min, max]`. |
| `math_max(a, b)` / `math_min(a, b)` | Larger/smaller of two numbers. |
| `math_pow(x, y)` | `x` raised to the power `y`. |
| `math_sqrt(x)` | Square root. |
| `math_exp(x)` / `math_log(x)` / `math_log10(x)` | `e^x`, natural log, base-10 log. |
| `math_sin(x)` / `math_cos(x)` / `math_tan(x)` | Trigonometric functions (radians). |
| `math_sinh(x)` / `math_cosh(x)` / `math_tanh(x)` | Hyperbolic variants. |
| `math_asin(x)` / `math_acos(x)` / `math_atan(x)` / `math_atan2(y, x)` | Inverse trig functions. |
| `math_deg(x)` / `math_rad(x)` | Radians ⇄ degrees conversion. |
| `math_fmod(x, y)` | Floating-point remainder of `x / y`. |
| `math_modf(x)` | Splits `x` into `[integer_part, fractional_part]`. |
| `math_frexp(x)` | Splits `x` into `[exponent, mantissa]` (see C's `frexp`). |
| `math_ldexp(x, n)` | `x * 2^n`. |
| `math_pi()` | Returns π. |
| `math_flt_epsilon()` | Returns `FLT_EPSILON`. |
| `math_maxval()` | Returns `DBL_MAX`. |
| `math_random()` | With no arguments, a float in `[0, 1)`. |
| `math_random(n)` | An integer in `[1, n]`. |
| `math_random(min, max)` | An integer in `[min, max]`. |
| `math_randomseed([seed])` | Seeds the RNG (Mersenne Twister); uses the current time if `seed` is omitted. |

```
math_clamp(150, 0, 100);   # 100
math_random(1, 6);          # simulate a die roll
```

### 2.5 Crypto (namespaced under `math_*`)

These live under the `math_` prefix in the C source and the global
namespace — there is no separate `crypto_*` prefix, despite crypto being
its own conceptual category.

| Function | Description |
|---|---|
| `math_md5(s)` | Returns the MD5 hash of string `s` as a 32-character lowercase hex string. |
| `math_bcrypt_gen_salt(cost)` | Generates a bcrypt salt; `cost` (work factor) must be between 4 and 31. |
| `math_bcrypt_hashpw(password, salt)` | Hashes `password` with `salt` (from `math_bcrypt_gen_salt`, or an existing bcrypt hash to verify against). Returns the bcrypt hash string. |

```
let salt = math_bcrypt_gen_salt(12);
let hash = math_bcrypt_hashpw("hunter2", salt);
# ... later, to verify a login attempt:
let ok = (math_bcrypt_hashpw(attempt, hash) == hash);
```

### 2.6 I/O functions (`io_*`)

File I/O:

| Function | Description |
|---|---|
| `io_open(path[, mode])` | Opens a file (`mode` defaults to `"r"`, same modes as C `fopen`). Returns an opaque IO handle (`c_obj`). |
| `io_read()` | With no arguments, reads a line from stdin. |
| `io_read(handle)` | Reads the entire contents of an open file handle as a string. |
| `io_scan_line()` | Reads a single line from stdin (without the arbitrary-length growth semantics of `io_read()`). |
| `io_write(handle, value)` | Writes `value` (null/bool/number/string) to an open file handle. |
| `io_seek(handle, offset, whence)` | Seeks in an open file; `whence` is `"set"`, `"cur"` or `"end"`. |
| `io_close(handle)` | Closes an open file handle. |
| `io_rename(old, new)` | Renames a file. |
| `io_remove(path)` | Deletes a file. |
| `io_mkdir(path)` | Creates a directory. |
| `io_filetype(path)` | Returns `"file"`, `"directory"`, `"unknown"`, or `null` if `path` doesn't exist. |

TAR archive I/O (also used for FH's own `.fhpack` package format):

| Function | Description |
|---|---|
| `io_tar_open(path[, mode])` | Opens a tar archive (`mode` defaults to `"r"`). Returns an opaque tar handle. |
| `io_tar_read(handle, filename)` | Reads a file's contents out of an open tar archive. |
| `io_tar_list(handle)` | Returns an array of file names in the tar archive. |
| `io_tar_write_header(handle, filename, size)` | Writes a file header for the next entry (archive must be opened for writing). |
| `io_tar_write_data(handle, data)` | Writes `data` for the entry started by `io_tar_write_header`. |
| `io_tar_write_finalize(handle)` | Finalizes the archive (writes the terminating records). |
| `io_tar_close(handle)` | Closes the tar handle. |

### 2.7 OS / system functions (`os_*`)

| Function | Description |
|---|---|
| `os_time()` | Returns an opaque high-resolution timestamp object (`c_obj`), for use with `os_difftime`. |
| `os_difftime(t0, t1)` | Returns the number of microseconds between two `os_time()` objects. |
| `os_localtime()` | Returns the current local time as a human-readable string (C's `asctime`). |
| `os_command(cmd)` | Runs `cmd` through the system shell; returns `true` if it exited with status 0. |
| `os_getenv(name)` | Returns the value of environment variable `name`, or `null` if unset. |
| `os_getOS()` | Returns the host OS name: `"linux"`, `"macOS"`, `"windows"` or `"bsd"`. |

### 2.8 JSON (`json_*`)

| Function | Description |
|---|---|
| `json_parse(s)` | Parses a JSON string into FH values (objects → maps, arrays → arrays, numbers → int or float as appropriate). |
| `json_stringify(v, [pretty])` | Serializes an FH value (null/bool/number/string/array/map) to a JSON string. `pretty` defaults to `false`, which gives compact, single-line output; pass `true` for indented output, which is what you want whenever a human or a diff tool reads the result. |

```
let data = json_parse('{"name": "John", "scores": [95, 87]}');
data["name"];                 # "John"
data["scores"][0];             # 95
json_stringify({"a": 1, "b": true});   # '{"a":1,"b":true}'

json_stringify({"a": 1}, true);
# {
#     "a":    1
# }
```

### 2.9 Garbage collector (`gc*`)

| Function | Description |
|---|---|
| `gc()` | Forces an immediate garbage collection. |
| `gc_info()` | Returns the number of bytes currently allocated. |
| `gc_pause(bool)` | Pauses (`true`) or resumes (`false`) automatic collection. |
| `gc_frequency(bytes)` | Sets the allocation threshold (bytes, `>= 1000000`) that triggers an automatic collection. |

